WEBVTT

1
00:00:23.943 --> 00:00:29.932
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL MOOCs course on Foundations for Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:41.468
We are going to start our next lecture, that is lecture number 5, which will be covering issues and challenges that are there in virtual and augmented reality systems.

3
00:00:43.371 --> 00:00:51.503
Before we start, like we were doing in the previous lectures, let us quickly recap what we have covered so far.

4
00:00:52.934 --> 00:01:08.221
So, we started our discussion with the basic understanding of the terms, broadly what they mean, what they refer to, and we also had a brief look at the importance,

4.1
00:01:08.221 --> 00:01:20.123
why this knowledge is important, why we should know about these terms, these technologies, primarily from the point of view of the commercial,

5
00:01:20.845 --> 00:01:26.955
potential applications and market caps.

6
00:01:27.536 --> 00:01:34.307
So, essentially from a commercial standpoint, we tried to understand why this is important.

7
00:01:35.309 --> 00:01:38.454
In the subsequent lectures, we went a little deeper.

8
00:01:39.215 --> 00:01:46.768
We explained the terms in terms of formal definitions of all the terms that we have come across.

9
00:01:48.217 --> 00:01:52.384
namely virtual reality, augmented reality and mixed reality.

10
00:01:54.567 --> 00:02:08.310
Also, you may recollect that we used the term augmented reality; we decided to use the terms augmented reality and mixed reality as synonyms.

11
00:02:08.911 --> 00:02:11.355
So, they are used interchangeably.

12
00:02:13.141 --> 00:02:18.609
which we are going to follow in all our discussions in subsequent lectures.

13
00:02:19.891 --> 00:02:24.778
Then we covered again in brief some major application areas.

14
00:02:26.641 --> 00:02:40.802
While we are talking about application areas, application domains of individual technologies, we also had seen the characteristics, key characteristics, key defining characteristics we can say

15
00:02:41.423 --> 00:02:43.065
of each of these technologies.

16
00:02:43.685 --> 00:02:55.873
Now, these defining characteristics could help us to distinguish between say a virtual reality system and

16.1
00:02:55.873 --> 00:03:05.608
a non-virtual reality system or say an augmented reality system or a non-augmented reality system.

17
00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:22.949
Not only that, along with these types of distinctions, we are also able to differentiate between a virtual reality system and an augmented reality system if we know those characteristics properly.

18
00:03:26.234 --> 00:03:32.743
So, we covered a few of the concepts in detail during those discussions.

19
00:03:32.993 --> 00:03:42.405
In the last lecture, we learned about the historical evolution of the field, both virtual reality and augmented reality.

20
00:03:42.925 --> 00:03:51.296
We covered key milestones that occurred during this journey from the very beginning to the current state of the art.

21
00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.122
That is what we have covered so far.

22
00:03:57.463 --> 00:04:01.228
Now, in this lecture, what we are going to do is we

23
00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:12.075
are going to talk about some issues and challenges while dealing with such systems, as is the convention that we are following.

24
00:04:12.376 --> 00:04:23.512
So, we will start our discussion on issues and challenges for virtual reality systems and in subsequent parts of the lecture, we are going to cover the same thing for augmented reality systems.

25
00:04:23.872 --> 00:04:29.781
So, let us begin our discussion on the key issues and challenges in virtual reality systems.

26
00:04:30.402 --> 00:04:34.087
or for developing or working with virtual reality systems.

27
00:04:37.993 --> 00:04:48.127
In order to understand the issues and challenges, let us quickly recollect the definition of virtual reality.

28
00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:59.363
We will be referring to the dictionary definition that we used in our earlier discussion on virtual reality.

29
00:04:59.815 --> 00:05:03.180
What definition did we use there? Let us quickly recollect.

30
00:05:04.782 --> 00:05:13.774
A virtual reality system is an artificial environment which is experienced through sensory stimuli.

31
00:05:16.398 --> 00:05:28.995
Note that here we are referring to only two sensory organs to understand sight and to perceive sight and sound, but in reality we can actually

32
00:05:29.498 --> 00:05:32.482
for full immersion we need to use all our sensory organs.

33
00:05:35.906 --> 00:05:50.684
So, an artificial environment which is experienced through sensory stimuli provided by a computer and in which one's actions partially determine what happens in the environment.

34
00:05:50.704 --> 00:05:54.409
So, earlier we went through this definition

35
00:05:54.642 --> 00:06:02.531
and we broke it down into key characteristics of a virtual reality system.

36
00:06:02.551 --> 00:06:04.674
So, no need to go into that discussion any further.

37
00:06:05.155 --> 00:06:17.750
However, what we can say is that based on this definition, we can say what is the objective, what is the main objective of any VR system.

38
00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:23.957
So, the objective is to let the user experience

39
00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:31.220
an artificial environment through sensory stimuli provided by the computer.

40
00:06:31.761 --> 00:06:41.755
So, here we are of course extending the definition a little bit instead of only sight and sound we are saying all sensory organs we are referring to all sensory organs.

41
00:06:42.897 --> 00:06:51.970
So, the overall objective of any VR system we can say is to let the user experience the artificial environment

42
00:06:52.658 --> 00:06:58.023
through our sensory organs or through the stimuli provided by the computer.

43
00:06:59.965 --> 00:07:04.430
That is the objective.

44
00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:06.112
Now, it may appear to be very simple.

45
00:07:06.392 --> 00:07:17.623
However, this objective actually refers to two important things which are nothing but the main issues in experiencing VR.

46
00:07:20.926 --> 00:07:21.467
So, there are

47
00:07:21.886 --> 00:07:24.609
broadly two major issues in experiencing VR.

48
00:07:25.831 --> 00:07:26.852
One is the technological.

49
00:07:28.874 --> 00:07:34.821
So, we said that we should be able to experience it through sensory stimuli provided by a computer.

50
00:07:35.341 --> 00:07:48.857
So, the technological issue is how to generate suitable stimuli to experience the virtual environment or artificial environment, but it does not end there.

51
00:07:49.097 --> 00:07:51.640
So, there is one more issue to be considered.

52
00:07:52.143 --> 00:07:52.704
What is that?

53
00:07:53.284 --> 00:07:54.786
That is psychological.

54
00:07:54.806 --> 00:08:05.197
Now, we are saying that through technology, somehow we manage to generate some stimuli, suitable stimuli in other words.

55
00:08:05.417 --> 00:08:10.483
Now, how to perceive that stimuli suitably?

56
00:08:11.283 --> 00:08:14.627
So, that is the psychological issue.

57
00:08:14.927 --> 00:08:16.229
Technology has nothing to do with it.

58
00:08:17.991 --> 00:08:21.154
So, essentially this refers to the role of the human factor,

59
00:08:21.623 --> 00:08:28.611
how our psychology is made up, our psychological characteristics.

60
00:08:30.033 --> 00:08:34.138
Of course, as you can understand, different people will have different characteristics.

61
00:08:34.198 --> 00:08:43.068
So this perception of stimuli is likely to be different for different users.

62
00:08:47.093 --> 00:08:50.938
So to sum up, there are two major issues.

63
00:08:52.403 --> 00:09:03.933
One is technological: how to generate stimuli, or rather how to generate suitable stimuli; and one is psychological: how to perceive the stimuli suitably.

64
00:09:09.278 --> 00:09:21.128
These two major issues in turn bring us to two major concerns, two important concerns while dealing with virtual reality system, technology, or experience.

65
00:09:22.711 --> 00:09:32.303
These are concerns that you will come across in almost every literature that deals with such technology.

66
00:09:32.323 --> 00:09:35.668
One is called immersion; the other one is called presence.

67
00:09:36.328 --> 00:09:38.932
Now, let us try to understand what these terms mean.

68
00:09:39.432 --> 00:09:46.862
Again, we will go through some definitions and we will try to understand based on the definitions what these terms mean.

69
00:09:51.297 --> 00:09:55.183
Let us begin our discussion with immersion.

70
00:09:55.203 --> 00:09:56.925
What does immersion mean?

71
00:10:02.033 --> 00:10:11.287
Like we have done before while defining virtual reality, let us similarly begin with a definition of immersion.

72
00:10:11.667 --> 00:10:20.781
It is not a dictionary definition; rather, this is a definition taken from the literature which says:

73
00:10:21.419 --> 00:10:51.122
Immersion is the objective degree to which a VR system and application projects stimuli onto the sensory receptors of users in a way that is extensive, matching, surrounding, vivid, interactive and

74
00:10:51.777 --> 00:10:55.621
plot informing.

75
00:10:55.641 --> 00:10:56.722
So, what does it say?

76
00:10:56.983 --> 00:11:00.146
It says that immersion is the objective degree.

77
00:11:01.568 --> 00:11:02.248
A degree of what?

78
00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:19.607
Objective degree to which a VR system and application projects stimuli onto our or the human user's sensory receptors in a way such that

79
00:11:19.891 --> 00:11:33.227
The experience is extensive, matching, surrounding, vivid, interactive and plot informing.

80
00:11:33.247 --> 00:11:38.594
This is a definition which was originally proposed by Slater and Wilbur.

81
00:11:39.194 --> 00:11:42.338
I will give you the reference at the end of this lecture.

82
00:11:47.583 --> 00:11:59.122
So, the terms to note in the definition are objective degree, VR system and application,

82.1
00:11:59.122 --> 00:12:14.299
projects stimuli, sensory receptors of users and of course, the seven terms namely extensive, matching, surrounding, vivid,

83
00:12:14.819 --> 00:12:22.395
interactive and plot informing, these six terms.

84
00:12:22.415 --> 00:12:35.342
So, in a nutshell, we can say that immersion is the objective degree which decides the qualities of experience,

85
00:12:36.031 --> 00:12:39.335
That a user is likely to have while dealing with a VR system.

86
00:12:40.076 --> 00:12:55.493
And these qualities are encapsulated in terms of these six terms: extensive, matching, surrounding, vivid, interactive and plot informing.

87
00:12:55.513 --> 00:12:58.917
Let us try to understand what these qualitative terms mean.

88
00:12:59.818 --> 00:13:03.102
So, let us begin with the first term, extensive.

89
00:13:06.592 --> 00:13:07.353
So, what does it mean?

90
00:13:07.633 --> 00:13:13.059
It means the range of sensory modalities that are presented to the user.

91
00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:26.473
The more sensory organs are stimulated by the stimuli provided by the computer, the more extensive the immersion is.

92
00:13:26.733 --> 00:13:30.557
That is what this term refers to.

93
00:13:32.647 --> 00:13:34.949
What can be the sensory organs?

94
00:13:34.969 --> 00:13:46.100
So, we have visual organs, auditory organs, physical force perception, smell, taste, touch, and so on.

95
00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:56.589
So, when we are talking about immersive experience, the more sensory organs we are using, the more extensive the experience is.

96
00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:58.792
That is what this term means.

97
00:14:00.954 --> 00:14:02.535
Then comes the second term.

98
00:14:02.718 --> 00:14:03.218
Matching.

99
00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:06.321
What does it mean?

100
00:14:07.642 --> 00:14:12.787
So, it refers to congruence between sensory modalities.

101
00:14:14.589 --> 00:14:26.920
For example, appropriate visual presentation corresponding to head motion and virtual representation of one's own body should be there.

102
00:14:28.882 --> 00:14:32.725
So, when you are moving, say, your head,

103
00:14:33.312 --> 00:14:39.561
Something in your visual field should move instantly without any delay.

104
00:14:39.862 --> 00:14:47.674
Otherwise, you will not get the perception that you are actually moving your head or your field of view.

105
00:14:49.216 --> 00:14:59.151
And this type of match between the user's physical movement and the movement in the virtual world is called matching.

106
00:15:03.350 --> 00:15:12.483
So, matching should be there while you are walking in the virtual world through some movement in the physical world or

106.1
00:15:12.483 --> 00:15:21.270
changing your field of view as I just explained or say moving your hand in the physical world.

107
00:15:21.731 --> 00:15:30.100
So, similar things which let you perceive that you are moving your hand should happen in the virtual world and so on and so forth.

108
00:15:34.349 --> 00:15:37.111
Then comes the third quality, that is, surrounding.

109
00:15:38.092 --> 00:15:42.216
It is the extent to which cues are panoramic.

110
00:15:42.876 --> 00:15:46.440
The extent to which cues are panoramic.

111
00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:50.303
So, that is the meaning of this term, surrounding.

112
00:15:52.805 --> 00:16:02.854
In other words, the extent of, say, when we are talking of visual senses, extent of field of view,

113
00:16:04.049 --> 00:16:13.018
When we are talking of auditory senses, the extent of our perception about auditory source and so on.

114
00:16:13.238 --> 00:16:27.331
So, wide field of view as compared to narrow field of view refers to more surrounding experience as compared to less surrounding experience.

115
00:16:27.531 --> 00:16:33.297
So, when I use the term wide field of view, I am essentially referring to the quality, more surrounding

116
00:16:33.817 --> 00:16:37.381
as compared to when I am using the term narrow field of view.

117
00:16:38.041 --> 00:16:40.804
So, that refers to the quality, less surrounding.

118
00:16:45.789 --> 00:16:52.095
Then vivid, what does it mean?

119
00:16:52.856 --> 00:16:57.921
The quality of energy stimulated within a particular modality.

120
00:16:57.961 --> 00:17:02.305
So, earlier we talked about using different modalities.

121
00:17:02.639 --> 00:17:11.127
Now, the vivid quality refers to the fact that within each of those modalities how much energy we are stimulating that means how

121.1
00:17:11.127 --> 00:17:16.133
much energy the computer is generating to stimulate that sensory organ.

122
00:17:19.336 --> 00:17:32.410
So, this quality is related to factors such as resolution, lighting, frame rate, audio bit rate; the more the better, as is obvious.

123
00:17:33.014 --> 00:17:37.620
So, if we have more resolution, then our visual perception would be better.

124
00:17:38.161 --> 00:17:41.566
If we have more audio bitrate, then our auditory perception will be better.

125
00:17:42.848 --> 00:17:51.419
If we have more lighting conditions, varied lighting conditions, brighter lights, then our visual perception will be better.

126
00:17:52.140 --> 00:18:01.333
So, vividness means these types of things that enhance our

127
00:18:02.528 --> 00:18:06.554
perceptions through each of the sensory modalities.

128
00:18:12.042 --> 00:18:21.958
In other words, we can say that more resolution means more vivid immersion, more bit rate means more vivid immersion, and so on.

129
00:18:26.144 --> 00:18:30.190
Then comes the quality of interactivity.

130
00:18:30.659 --> 00:18:34.304
So when I say interaction or interactive quality, what does it mean?

131
00:18:36.246 --> 00:18:45.561
Among these six, this is probably the most obvious one, that is the capability of the user to make changes to the world,

131.1
00:18:45.561 --> 00:18:56.032
response of virtual entities to user actions, and the user's ability to influence future events.

132
00:18:57.173 --> 00:18:59.957
Interactivity in a nutshell refers to

133
00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:09.314
Our ability, or rather the user's ability to interact with the virtual world, is not only one-way interaction to understand the response,

133.1
00:19:09.314 --> 00:19:17.102
but also the ability to change future events, the ability to influence future events.

134
00:19:22.432 --> 00:19:30.960
So, if you recollect in our definition of VR, we said one of the defining characteristics of any VR system is interactivity.

135
00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:33.102
So, that is also reflected here.

136
00:19:37.446 --> 00:19:43.231
Finally, come to the last quality, which is plot informing.

137
00:19:43.251 --> 00:19:45.233
Now, this is very interesting.

138
00:19:45.613 --> 00:19:50.818
So, what this quality refers to is that through our interaction or other qualities,

139
00:19:51.372 --> 00:20:01.945
whether we are getting a feel of how things are happening, how things will happen in the future.

140
00:20:02.245 --> 00:20:09.113
I mean the consequences of our actions, whether we are able to understand, comprehend, or predict the consequences.

141
00:20:09.594 --> 00:20:15.781
In other words, it refers to the story, the story of the entire experience.

142
00:20:15.801 --> 00:20:18.865
So, here it is assumed that the experience is

143
00:20:19.503 --> 00:20:22.245
nothing but a story, a scripted story.

144
00:20:23.666 --> 00:20:34.816
As the scripts, as the story, I mean, each of the instances changes through user interactions, and the story unfolds.

145
00:20:39.079 --> 00:20:49.408
The plot informing quality refers to our ability to understand the overall story, not only the current instance of what is happening, but the overall story, the overall script.

146
00:20:50.131 --> 00:20:58.243
The more we are able to apprehend the script, the story, the more plot-informing the experience is.

147
00:21:03.470 --> 00:21:17.590
So, it refers to the story, which, in other words, refers to the consistent portrayal of a message or experience, dynamic unfolding of sequences of events,

148
00:21:18.346 --> 00:21:26.909
behavior of the world and its entities, whether the user is able to understand, comprehend, or predict those things,

148.1
00:21:26.909 --> 00:21:33.371
that is collectively we can say is referring to the understanding of the story.

149
00:21:35.114 --> 00:21:44.910
So, the more we understand the story, the more plot informing it is; the less we understand the story, the less plot informing the experience is.

150
00:21:49.109 --> 00:21:56.120
Now, that was about immersion, which is the technological aspect or technological issue for VR.

151
00:21:56.641 --> 00:22:01.107
So, just to summarize, there are two issues: technological and psychological.

152
00:22:02.089 --> 00:22:06.355
And correspondingly, there are two terms we refer to: immersion and presence.

153
00:22:06.856 --> 00:22:08.639
Immersion refers to the technological issue.

154
00:22:10.281 --> 00:22:15.910
When we say we want to immerse or we want to generate,

155
00:22:16.430 --> 00:22:27.163
an immersive experience for the user, essentially we are referring to the technological side or the systemic side, which is captured in terms of these six qualities.

156
00:22:28.724 --> 00:22:34.391
And immersion refers to the objective degree to which these qualities are imparted to the user.

157
00:22:35.372 --> 00:22:42.781
And these qualities refer to activation of one or more sensory organs in a suitable way.

158
00:22:43.381 --> 00:22:44.783
Now, that is the

159
00:22:45.522 --> 00:22:46.604
what technology can do.

160
00:22:49.087 --> 00:22:52.653
Let us come to the psychological side, that is presence.

161
00:22:54.135 --> 00:23:02.221
We may generate immersive qualities, but whether we will be able to perceive that or not, or whether the

161.1
00:23:02.221 --> 00:23:09.618
user will be able to perceive that or not, that is another issue which is captured by this term presence.

162
00:23:12.082 --> 00:23:13.504
What is the definition, says?

163
00:23:14.142 --> 00:23:18.286
So, this definition is taken from the International Society for Presence Research.

164
00:23:18.307 --> 00:23:22.091
It was there since 2000.

165
00:23:23.792 --> 00:23:42.833
What it says is, presence is a psychological state or subjective perception in which even though part or all of an individual's current experience is generated by and/or filtered through

166
00:23:43.454 --> 00:23:55.312
human-made technology, part or all of the individual's perception fails to accurately acknowledge the role of the technology in the experience.

167
00:23:55.332 --> 00:23:57.635
So, it is a little bit complicated.

168
00:23:59.558 --> 00:24:00.920
Let me go through it again.

169
00:24:01.681 --> 00:24:09.833
A psychological state or subjective perception, that is the first thing to note, in which even though

170
00:24:10.910 --> 00:24:26.104
part or all of an individual's current experience is, so whatever the individual is currently experiencing, either partly or fully, is generated by or filtered through

170.1
00:24:26.104 --> 00:24:40.461
human-made technology; part or all of the individual's perception, so whatever we are perceiving at that moment, fails to accurately acknowledge

171
00:24:41.065 --> 00:24:49.499
fails to accurately acknowledge, note this term, the role of the technology in the experience.

172
00:24:55.348 --> 00:25:03.902
So, essentially what it means is that it is a psychological state, number one.

173
00:25:05.064 --> 00:25:08.389
Number two, in this state, we are

174
00:25:09.263 --> 00:25:16.711
getting input from technology, from computers, which is stimulating our sensory organs.

175
00:25:17.192 --> 00:25:18.874
So, we are perceiving something.

176
00:25:19.695 --> 00:25:31.868
However, presence means that our perception should be as good as perceiving reality.

177
00:25:31.888 --> 00:25:38.275
That means we should not be able to distinguish between our perception of

178
00:25:39.267 --> 00:25:44.574
stimuli generated by a computer vis-à-vis our perception of stimuli generated from the real world.

179
00:25:45.034 --> 00:25:48.198
We should not be able to distinguish between the two.

180
00:25:49.640 --> 00:25:57.590
If that type of perception is happening, then we say that we are having a presence in the environment.

181
00:25:59.252 --> 00:26:08.143
So, in other words, we can say that the computer is generating stimuli which are stimulating our sensory organs, but we fail to acknowledge that

182
00:26:08.343 --> 00:26:13.188
that these are generated by a computer; instead, we perceive them to be coming from the real world.

183
00:26:13.428 --> 00:26:18.152
So, in other words, we are perceiving that we are already in the real world; nothing changed.

184
00:26:18.973 --> 00:26:35.228
That means we are unable to distinguish between the virtual world and the real world; that is presence, or rather, to say, perfect presence.

185
00:26:36.271 --> 00:26:39.295
How are these two related: immersion and presence?

186
00:26:39.856 --> 00:26:47.955
Immersion can be an objective and quantifiable description of what any particular system can provide or does provide,

186.1
00:26:47.955 --> 00:26:59.205
whereas presence is a state of consciousness or the psychological sense of being in the virtual environment.

187
00:26:59.225 --> 00:27:03.872
So, immersion is what the technology gives us.

188
00:27:04.653 --> 00:27:05.434
We can measure

189
00:27:06.005 --> 00:27:12.035
or we can comprehend what the technology can provide.

190
00:27:12.576 --> 00:27:17.825
However, when we are talking about presence, it is inside the user's mind.

191
00:27:19.929 --> 00:27:22.754
There is no direct manifestation of it.

192
00:27:22.774 --> 00:27:24.938
It is difficult to objectively measure.

193
00:27:25.519 --> 00:27:29.245
So, subjective experience has to be taken into account.

194
00:27:30.912 --> 00:27:36.681
That is the major difference between these two concepts: immersion and presence.

195
00:27:40.687 --> 00:27:53.146
Now, as is probably obvious by now, presence can be a function of both the user, that is the subjective experience, as well as immersion, that is the objective or technological experience.

196
00:27:53.847 --> 00:27:57.392
So, immersion aids presence, but it does not mean that

197
00:27:58.148 --> 00:28:01.312
if we provide immersive technology, then presence will automatically happen.

198
00:28:05.438 --> 00:28:10.806
Immersion is capable of producing presence, but not necessarily always.

199
00:28:11.046 --> 00:28:15.592
Say, for example, you are asking a user to experience a virtual reality system.

200
00:28:15.813 --> 00:28:21.841
You ask the user to wear the headset and do all the things, all the interactions that are supported by the system.

201
00:28:23.383 --> 00:28:26.788
But if the user simply keeps the eyes shut, then

202
00:28:27.072 --> 00:28:36.184
and imagines that he or she is in some other place, then all these technological things will come to naught.

203
00:28:36.204 --> 00:28:36.885
It will mean nothing.

204
00:28:38.347 --> 00:28:46.118
So, the user can simply shut their eyes and cut themselves off from that virtual experience.

205
00:28:47.420 --> 00:28:51.966
So, immersion is still there, but presence will not be there.

206
00:28:55.422 --> 00:28:58.145
On the other hand, presence can be limited by immersion.

207
00:28:59.186 --> 00:29:07.255
The greater immersion a system or application provides, the greater potential for a user to feel present in that virtual world.

208
00:29:08.056 --> 00:29:13.301
The lesser the immersive technology provides, the lesser is the presence.

209
00:29:14.222 --> 00:29:14.763
That is there.

210
00:29:15.023 --> 00:29:24.433
However, as we have just seen, immersion does not ensure presence, but immersion can aid enhanced perception of presence.

211
00:29:27.046 --> 00:29:49.518
So with this knowledge, we can say that the overall goal of any virtual reality system is to create a high degree of presence, or in other words, to make users feel that they are really in the virtual world,

211.1
00:29:49.518 --> 00:29:55.517
but they are unable to distinguish between the real world and the virtual world.

212
00:29:56.780 --> 00:30:03.468
So earlier we said the goal of a VR system is to give an immersive experience.

213
00:30:03.589 --> 00:30:12.239
Now we are refining and saying that the goal is to create a high degree of presence because now we know what presence is and what immersion is.

214
00:30:12.800 --> 00:30:20.830
Now to create a high degree of presence, we have to have a system that provides a high degree of immersion, and that is a systemic issue.

215
00:30:24.303 --> 00:30:38.175
So with that, we have come to the end of this part of the lecture where we have covered a few important concepts, namely the technological challenge and the concept of immersion,

215.1
00:30:38.175 --> 00:30:43.553
the psychological challenge and the concept of presence and how they are related, how they are different,

215.1.1
00:30:43.553 --> 00:30:52.757
what are the things to be considered while creating an immersive experience and what is the overall goal of any VR system.

216
00:30:55.623 --> 00:31:04.253
But that is not all; immersion and presence are not the only issues that are afflicting any VR experience.

217
00:31:04.973 --> 00:31:10.440
There are other issues as well, and there are issues for AR systems also.

218
00:31:10.900 --> 00:31:14.424
Those we will be taking up in the next part of the lecture.

219
00:31:18.168 --> 00:31:24.195
And these are the references from where those terms were taken and definitions were taken.

220
00:31:26.858 --> 00:31:32.626
If you go through them, you will get to know more about these concepts.

221
00:31:34.869 --> 00:31:39.094
The second one, that is Slater and Wilbur, 1997.

222
00:31:39.114 --> 00:31:43.740
This is one of the earliest papers related to this topic.

223
00:31:45.202 --> 00:31:54.975
And I strongly suggest that you go through this reference to get a better understanding of how things were dealt with at that point in time.

224
00:31:57.790 --> 00:32:00.116
So, with that, we have come to the end of this lecture.

225
00:32:00.136 --> 00:32:01.760
I hope you have enjoyed it.

226
00:32:01.780 --> 00:32:06.553
We will cover the other things related to issues and challenges in the next part.

227
00:32:07.375 --> 00:32:08.759
Until then, goodbye.

228
00:32:08.799 --> 00:32:11.125
Thank you, and happy learning.
